WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:18.080
Despite huge differences in morphology and
biological structures, all living organisms

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.780
do the same three basic things;

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:25.420
they get food, digest it, and excrete waste materials.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.490
Living organisms require energy to live.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.440
Some produce their own food, usually through
photosynthesis, we call these autotrophs,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.150
but many organisms cannot make their own food.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:44.620
We call these heterotrophs and they...eat.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:50.800
Among heterotrophic single-celled eukaryotes,
food is taken into the cell in various methods

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.940
but once it’s there, it's wrapped by
a membrane

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.550
and forms something called a food vacuole.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:04.980
Then the cell flushes digestive enzymes inside
the food vacuole to start the digestion.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:09.900
Nutrients are taken into the cytoplasm and
the waste material left in the vacuole then

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.600
basically fuses with the outer
membrane of the cell and what's left in the

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.060
vacuole is discharged into the environment

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:24.220
In some cases, like inside this beautiful
single-celled Nassula ornata which feeds on

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:29.940
filamentous cyanobacteria, content of the
food vacuole reacts with the digestive enzymes

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.280
and changes color.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.600
But that process takes time.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:38.900
Because each vacuole formed at a different
time, they are in different stages of digestion

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.720
which gives this cell it’s colorful polka
dots.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.050
But how do these heterotrophic organisms get their food?

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.320
Well, in spite of a remarkable
amount of diversity, a lot of microorganisms

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:57.540
use one of the same three strategies for getting
their food.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.400
Some of this will be familiar to the macro world

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.430
some of it will not.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.960
One of the less familiar is filter feeding

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:14.860
which allows larger organisms to consume suspended
food particles or much smaller organisms.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:19.420
There are filter-feeders in the macro-world,
baleen whales come to mind.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:26.080
But while a whale must swim through giant
clouds of small organisms, in the microcosmos,

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.220
your food can come to you.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:36.380
Some filter feeders use hair-like structures called cilia to create a vortex that brings

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:41.360
other microorganisms or food particles to
the cell mouth.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:47.090
These cilia are specialized for this task
and their beating creates a current that expediently

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:53.230
and beautifully directs every nearby thing
into the waiting mouth of the microorganism.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:58.910
The cilia are often too small for us to see,
but you can see their effects.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:03.500
Take a look at these Paramecia. They're consuming tiny, tiny bacteria.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:08.760
and you can see their cilia causing small organisms to tumble across them.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.960
You can also see all of their food vacuoles on the inside,

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.732
and if you look very carefully, you can see a new food vacuole forming,

00:03:16.732 --> 00:03:20.160
getting filled up with those tiny, bacterial cells.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:30.640
Some of the best and most obvious filter feeders
are rotifers, micro-animals that use cilia

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:34.450
to create swirling vortices around their mouth parts.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:40.220
You can see how successful this feeding strategy
is by it’s belly full of algae cells.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:46.160
Every time its mouth fills with more algea,
it contracts to swallow the food.

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:52.180
Now observe these single-celled organisms called stentors,

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.880
they are much bigger than most other microorganisms.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:57.900
You can actually see them with the naked eye.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:04.000
and they also use filter feeding to push all of their algal food into their cell mouths.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.560
(Ominous music)

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.120
Our second feeding mechanism, maybe the most familiar,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.480
and the most exciting is called raptorial feeding.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:24.780
Raptorial feeders selectively capture prey
and hunt other organisms.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:28.020
In this video you can see Dileptus hunting.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:35.560
It paralyzes one organism with the touch of its trunk-like proboscis, and then it pulls that organism into itself

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:38.720
in a process called phagocytosis.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.600
Many of these microorganisms are armed with
something called toxicysts.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:52.729
These are little harpoon-like structures filled
with toxins and they are located on a particular

00:04:52.729 --> 00:04:57.089
part of the cell which the microorganism uses for hunting.

00:04:57.089 --> 00:05:02.360
These tiny harpoons are then fired when they come in contact with prey organisms

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.560
which then become immobilized.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.229
This is Bursaria, it’s a single-celled organism
with a huge mouth, and things have not gone

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:14.659
well for the Paramecium that is now inside it.

00:05:14.659 --> 00:05:21.280
The Paramecium dies immediately because of the toxicysts on the inside of the Bursaria,

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.560
so at least it was quick.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:31.789
Now get ready for some truly gorgeous footage of a micro-animal’s day going south.

00:05:31.789 --> 00:05:38.569
First Paradileptus immobilizes the rotifer with fired toxicysts and the animal is swallowed by

00:05:38.569 --> 00:05:41.680
the single-celled organism as it swims away.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.960
This is a Frontonia which is a close relative
of Paramecia, but lacks the filter-feeding

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:59.169
habits of its relatives and feeds predaciously
on large diatoms and filamentous cyanobacteria

00:05:59.169 --> 00:06:01.220
such as Oscillatoria here.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:08.759
Though, in this case, it turns out this Frontonia
bit off a little more than he could chew.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.160
Another raptorial feeding style is called
histophagy.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:19.430
Histophagous organisms such as these single-celled
Coleps attack injured but live animals or

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:26.000
other single-celled organisms, sucking off
hunks of tissue rather than consuming whole organisms.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.439
When they attack an animal, they enter wounds and ingest tissue often attacking in groups

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:37.160
because their chemical sensing abilities attract many of them from a distance,

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:39.960
like microscopic vultures.

00:06:39.979 --> 00:06:46.839
When a number of them gather in one place, it’s hard to avoid another macro-world analog...piranhas,

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:51.160
devouring everything soft in no time at all.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:59.430
There is a huge variety of raptorial feeding,
this is just the beginning, but we wanted to

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:02.490
show you one more before we move on.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:06.649
This is Vampyrella, an amoeba with a suitable
name.

00:07:06.649 --> 00:07:10.179
It specializes in feeding on filamentous
algae.

00:07:10.179 --> 00:07:21.000
First, it bores a hole through the algal cell
wall and then slurps out the gooey, nutritious cytoplasm

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.600
Our final feeding mechanism, for today at least,

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.440
is diffusion feeding,

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:36.039
in which the predator just sits in the same place, relying

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.600
on the prey to accidentally make contact.

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:45.720
This is a Heliozoa, it’s a single celled
amoeboid and because of its resemblance to

00:07:45.729 --> 00:07:52.939
the sun due to the rays coming out of its
cell, it’s sometimes called the “sun animalcule”.

00:07:52.939 --> 00:08:01.319
The rays are called axopodia. These are sometimes used in locomotion and, in this case, for hunting prey.

00:08:01.319 --> 00:08:07.919
Axopodia, are cytoplasmic extensions, meaning
they’re a part of the cell membrane, even

00:08:07.919 --> 00:08:10.120
though they look like they’re sticking out
of it.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:12.309
Each one has a central supporting

00:08:12.309 --> 00:08:16.940
rod of microtubules that gives it this rigid structure.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:23.419
The axopodia are coated in organelles that
discharge toxins when touched, which impair

00:08:23.419 --> 00:08:27.009
or even paralyze Heliozoa's prey.

00:08:27.009 --> 00:08:33.750
After the organism is captured, those microtubules
are drawn back into the cell, thus retracting

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:41.440
the axopodia and allowing the cell to swallow
the unlucky organism, or prey is just engulfed

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.500
by extrusions from the cell called pseudopodia.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:51.130
In this video, a rotifer has been captured
by a Heliozoa and it is slowly getting eaten by it.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:54.360
Now this is something that happens fairly freuently,

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:57.560
but we did capture something unusal here.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:05.440
While it was stuck to Heliozoa's axopodia, this rotifer actually lays its egg.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.760
But neither egg nor the rotifer is going to escape this.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:11.080
Surprisingly heartbreaking.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:18.840
This is a Suctorian, it is a ciliate just
like Paramecium and Stentor.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:24.600
These organisms have hair-like cilia during the early stage of their life, but as adults they

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.870
develop bundles of tentacles.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:33.700
Just like in Heliozoa these tentacles are
supported by an internal cylinder of microtubules.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:40.700
The tip of the tentacles have extrusomes;
these are special structures that attach to

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:44.440
and immobilize any other ciliates that touch them.

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:50.100
The tentacles eventually penetrate the cell
membrane of the prey, and then the contents

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:55.110
of the prey is sucked out through the tentacle.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:10:01.980
In the clip, a suctorian has caught 4 individual
Vorticella with its tentacles and is slowly

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:04.140
sucking their cytoplasm.

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:09.041
It looks a little like the vorticella have
the suctorian surrounded, but in fact, they

00:10:09.041 --> 00:10:11.130
are powerless to escape it.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:13.760
It’s a dangerous world out there.

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:20.050
The complex chemicals created by organisms
to sustain their life necessarily are useful

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:25.250
to other organisms, as building blocks and as fuel.

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:27.040
And so predation evolved.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:31.760
It’s beautiful, it’s constant, and it’s
brutal.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:35.120
Thank you for watching and for coming on this journey with us

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:38.720
as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:43.520
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James,

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.440
check out Jam's Germs on Instagram.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:48.000
And if you want to see more from us,

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:51.240
that, my friends, is what the "subscribe" button is for!

